Short:        Disk inspector, undelete and rescue
Author:       Paul Seibert (Desert Palms Software)
Uploader:     Paul Seibert <your@email.here>
Type:         disk/salv
Version:      1.0
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 1.3
Distribution: Aminet
Requires:     Nothing extra.

Dowser 1.0 - (c) 2026 Desert Palms Software. Written by Paul Seibert.
Freeware.

     "Dowser finds what's on your disks, and what was lost."

Dowser looks at a floppy, or at an .adf disk image, block by block. It
shows where everything is on a map of the disk, explains in plain English
what is wrong with it, lists the deleted files that can still be brought
back, and copies files off damaged disks.

It is written in C for AmigaOS 1.3 (V33) and runs unchanged on 2.x and
3.x. It only uses the 1.3 operating system, so it works on a stock A500
with 512K. It reads OFS and FFS disks, including the international and
directory-cache kinds (DOS\0 to DOS\5), even on 1.3, which cannot read
FFS floppies itself.

Dowser 1.0 is read-only: it never writes to the disk it is looking at.
Rescued files go to another drive or to RAM:.

The block map
-------------

 * All 1760 blocks of the disk, 80 cylinders by 22 blocks, each one drawn
   by what it is: boot block, root, bitmap, drawer, file header, file
   extension, data, directory cache, free, unreadable, lost (marked in
   use but used by nothing) and conflict (used twice, or used but marked
   free). A legend explains the patterns, which work on the four-colour
   Workbench screen.
 * Click a block and the info line says what it is and whose it is:
   "data block 3 of Work/readme.txt". Pick a file and all its blocks
   light up, so you can see how it is spread over the disk.

Check
-----

A list of findings in plain English, each with its block and file, and a
verdict at the top. It covers:

 * checksum errors, block by block
 * the bitmap against what is really used: blocks marked free that are in
   use (dangerous: the next file written may overwrite them), and blocks
   marked in use that nothing uses (lost space)
 * blocks used twice
 * broken hash chains and drawers: pointers off the disk, loops, the
   wrong kind of block, files in the wrong slot or with the wrong parent
 * OFS data blocks out of order, with the wrong owner or size, and files
   whose size does not match their blocks
 * a disk that needs validating
 * directory caches that disagree with their drawers
 * fragmentation, for the disk and for each file

Files and deleted files
-----------------------

 * Files: the whole tree with size, blocks, fragments and a status for
   each file (ok, damaged, unreadable).
 * Deleted: files that were deleted but are still on the disk, with the
   drawer they were in, each marked recoverable or partly overwritten
   (some of its blocks now belong to another file). It also finds
   orphans: files whose drawer, or the whole root block, has been lost.

Rescue
------

 * Copy a file, a drawer, the whole disk, a deleted file, or all the
   files found, to any drawer on another disk. Found files go back into
   the drawers they came from.
 * Names, dates, protection bits and comments are kept.
 * Dowser reads around damage: blocks that cannot be read, or that now
   belong to another file, come back as zeros, and Dowser says how many.
 * It works when AmigaDOS calls the disk "not a DOS disk": with a broken
   boot block, or without a root block, the tree is rebuilt from the file
   headers that are left.

Boot block
----------

 * DOS type and flags, checksum, bootable or not.
 * Standard Kickstart 1.x or 2.0+ install code, no boot code, or custom
   code. For custom code Dowser shows the readable text in it and its
   CRC32, so it can be looked up. It does not try to name viruses: a
   custom boot block may be a game loader, a utility or a virus, and
   Dowser says so honestly.

Block viewer and surface scan
-----------------------------

 * Any block in hex and ASCII, with its fields decoded for its type
   (header, data, extension, directory cache, bitmap, boot) and pointers
   turned into names. n and p step to the next and previous block.
 * Surface (drives only): every track read afresh, each one shown as
   good, damaged, or without AmigaDOS sectors (unformatted, or a custom
   format such as a copy-protected game). Unreadable tracks show on the
   map. Esc stops.

Installing and using
--------------------

Copy the Dowser drawer anywhere you like. Dowser needs no libraries,
fonts or assigns beyond the ROM.

Start it from Workbench, press a drive button (DF0: to DF3:) or Image to
open an .adf file, and the disk is read once, with a progress bar (Esc
stops). From the CLI you can name the disk and the tab straight away:

    dowser DF0:
    dowser Work:Disks/game.adf CHECK
    dowser DF1: DELETED

While Dowser reads a floppy, AmigaDOS is kept off that drive; it gets the
drive back afterwards.

There is also a disk image, dowser.adf, on the GitHub page: it boots
straight into Dowser.

Command mode
------------

"dowser CMD" runs Dowser's engine from the CLI or a script, without the
window: INFO, MAP, FILES, CHECK, BOOT, REPORT, DELETED, RESCUE, SCAN,
BLOCK and MEM. "dowser CMD" alone lists them. For example:

    dowser CMD CHECK DF0:
    dowser CMD DELETED Work:Disks/old.adf
    dowser CMD RESCUE DF0: DELETED ALL TO RAM:found
    dowser CMD RESCUE DF1: ALL TO Work:Rescued

Known limits
------------

 * 880K DD floppies and their images (HD disks and hard disk partitions
   are not read).
 * Read-only: Dowser 1.0 does not repair disks or edit blocks. Use it to
   get your files off first.
 * On a real floppy drive, damaged tracks are slow to read, because
   trackdisk.device retries them. Dowser remembers bad tracks so each is
   read only once.

Technical notes
---------------

 * Written in C, cross-compiled with GCC 6.5 (bebbo's amiga-gcc) against
   the Kickstart 1.3 includes, so it cannot call anything newer by
   accident. About 72K of 68000 code.
 * Tested under WinUAE on Kickstart 1.3 (A500, OCS, 512K + 512K) and
   Kickstart 3.1 (ECS, 1MB + 1MB) with automated test disks. Dowser's
   findings are compared with an independent reader written in Python,
   on OFS and FFS disks of every kind and on disks damaged on purpose in
   known ways. Rescued and undeleted files are compared byte for byte.
   The surface scan was tested on emulated drives holding damaged and
   empty disks. Every disk is checked to be unchanged afterwards, and no
   memory is lost.

